#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176617} :activity/id #uuid "7ae50821-e92a-574f-9179-08660135fa6f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049930 :topic/id #uuid "1f64bfc1-4120-531e-ba84-356d2ff12489" :topic/math-academy-id 3556} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177096 :tutorial/content "The graph of\n\n$$\n{y=2x+7}\n$$\n\nis shown in the picture below. Let's find the equation of the line that passes through $(4,-6)$ and is parallel to\n\n$$\ny=2x+7\n$$\n\n![](images/d8/d80d162b46c39cebddf07394219ec15f4bdbda94831cd66d22da8f285b680d61.png)\n\nSince parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of\n\n$$\ny=2x+7\n$$\n\nwhich is $2$.\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of $2$ and passes through the point $(4,-6)$. To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-6) &= 2(x - 4) \\\\\ny + 6 &= 2x - 8 \\\\\ny &= 2x - 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2e8f95a3-bef7-580d-877c-269f8df50256" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8901 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0a9f12e7-9444-5bd4-aef9-8e80d2d676fd" :step/math-academy-id 148506 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175910 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175736 :question/id #uuid "4dd86f6d-270f-5942-b7cf-df53a0bf4ee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-14361"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4dcbc520-40b5-5c08-ba56-be105c135240" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050635}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481545} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481504} {:db/id #edb/ref 175921